> Yes, by asking you whether you want to quit.

That in itself would be different from being stopped cold.  But of the
3 traditional Unix programs I mentioned (sh, ed, and vi), none of them
prompts you to quit.  It normally does nothing to sh, which passes it
along untouched to child programs (though it can be caught for
arbitrary behavior, or used to interrupt shell builtins).  In vi, it
stops any currently running mappings/abbrevs.  In ed, it aborts the
current line.

It's useful as an interrupt, the meaning of which may vary based on
the program's needs.  And in most civilized curses programs (vi,
emacs, pine, mutt, trn, slrn, etc) it does not just kill the program
dead.  It either prompts for exit or does something else intelligent.


